    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> were among the Three Crowned Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamilakam">Tamilakam</a> with the Chera and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a>. They appear in the Ashokan Edicts as neighbours of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangam-literature">Sangam literature</a> names early kings such as Karikala and Kocengannan, with Urayur as their oldest capital. After the Sangam age, around 300, the Kalabhras displaced the Tamil kingdoms, and the Cholas, reduced to chieftains, migrated to Telugu country, where Telugu Chola families claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karikala-chola">Karikala Chola</a>.</p><p>In the second quarter of the ninth century Vijayalaya, of the Pottapi Chola family, re-established the dynasty as independent rulers; the Chola Empire was founded in 848. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aditya-i">Aditya I</a> defeated the Pallava and Pandyan dynasties early in the 10th century, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tondaimandalam">Tondaimandalam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongu-nadu">Kongu Nadu</a>, and Aditya's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parantaka-i">Parantaka I</a> defeated the Rashtrakuta dynasty in the battle of Vallala and the Pandyas again. The heartland was the fertile valley of the Kaveri River.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> country lay in the flat plain of the Kaveri and its tributaries, now the districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>, Tiruchirapalli, Tiruvarur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagapattinam">Nagapattinam</a> and others. The river, also called the Ponni (Golden) river, had a special place in Chola culture, and its annual floods were celebrated as Adiperukku. Kaveripoompattinam near the delta and Nagappattinam were the major ports, which Ptolemy knew; they drew traders and several faiths, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and Roman coins of the early centuries CE have been found near the delta.</p><p>The other major towns were Thanjavur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraiyur">Uraiyur</a> and Kudanthai, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbakonam">Kumbakonam</a>; Thanjavur lost its importance after Rajendra Chola moved the capital to Gangaikonda Cholapuram. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-invasion-of-srivijaya">Chola raids</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> had large consequences for trade: Tamil traders entered the realm traditionally controlled by Malay traders, and the influence of the Tamil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> grew on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> and the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja I</a> and his successors the empire became a military power in South and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Rajaraja I's naval campaigns captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, the Maldives and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a>. Rajendra I conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> and the Pala dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, completed the conquest of Rajarata in Sri Lanka, and in 1025 launched naval raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and the Burmese kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>; an inscription says he captured or plundered 14 places, identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>, Tambralinga and Kedah among others. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virarajendra-chola">Virarajendra Chola</a> later conquered Kedah. The invasions meant to plunder, not to rule, but they gravely weakened Srivijayan hegemony.</p><p>On land the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> dominated the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> by war and tribute, fought the Eastern Gangas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> and the Sinhala kings of Rohana, and from 1150 faced constant warfare with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>. Kulothunga Chola III defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> generals at Karuvur and helped the Hoysalas end the Western Chalukyas between 1185 and 1190.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire was ruled by a hereditary dynasty whose line changed in 1070, when Athirajendra <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> was assassinated in a civil disturbance and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulottunga-i">Kulothunga Chola I</a>, son of the Eastern Chalukya prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-narendra">Rajaraja Narendra</a> and the Chola princess Ammanga Devi, took the throne, beginning the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-dynasty">Chola dynasty</a>. Marital alliances bound the Cholas to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a>, which remained largely independent under Chola protection, and Kulothunga Chola III allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii">Veera Ballala II</a> through a marriage.</p><p>Feudatories such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-cholas">Telugu Cholas</a> of Velanati and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nellore">Nellore</a> held the eastern coast for the Cholas, aided their campaigns and levied tribute on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> kingdoms. The Cholas were followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and were not swayed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> as the Pallava and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> kings had been. Under Rajadhiraja II and Kulothunga III the succession grew murky with disputes and intrigues.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> the Tamil country reached new heights in art, religion, music and literature; temples and sculpture in stone and bronze reached a finesse never before achieved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The Brihadeeswarar Temple was built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja I</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>-shaped Airavatesvara Temple at Dharasuram under Rajaraja Chola II. The era was a golden age of Tamil literature: Kamban flourished under Kulothunga III, Jayamkondar wrote the Kalingattuparani, Nambi Andar Nambi arranged the Shaivite canon into eleven books, and the Periapuranam, the Virasoliyam and the Nannul were written.</p><p>The conquest of Kadaram and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and continued contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> let the Cholas influence the cultures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>; the temple complex at Prambanan shows similarities with South Indian architecture. Rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> claimed Chola descent, and princes in Malaysia still bear names ending in Cholan or Chulan, such as Raja Chulan of Perak.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Around 1118 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> to the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangavadi">Gangavadi</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire</a>, setbacks soon reversed by Vikrama Chola. The empire was still largely intact under Rajaraja Chola II (1146–1175), and stable and prosperous until 1215 under Kulothunga Chola III, the last great Chola monarch. The decline began with his defeat by Maravarman Sundara Pandiyan II in 1215–16, after which the Cholas also lost Lanka to a revival of Sinhala power.</p><p>The Pandyan dynasty rose again as the most powerful rulers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>, and a Pandyan civil war drew in the Cholas and Sinhalas by proxy, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a> and the Pallavarayanpettai Inscriptions record. The rule of the later Cholas down to the last emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-iii">Rajendra Chola III</a>, was weaker than before, and the Cholas were absorbed by the Pandyan empire and ceased to exist by 1279.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, refounded by Vijayalaya in 848, made the Kaveri delta the centre of an empire that united southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and sent fleets against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, the Maldives and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>. Their reign was a golden age of Tamil temples, bronzes and literature, and their power faded after 1215 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> absorbed them by 1279.</em></p>

